# Following candidate comparisons
#
# Candidates can be more interesting than others because of a higher score,
# an award, a stated comparison, or a chain of such comparisons. The question
# here is whether A is more interesting than C. No single fact says so: A beats
# B on score, and B is stated to beat C, so the recursive backward rule chains
# the two. A log:query selects that answer as the output.

@prefix : <http://example.org/#> .
@prefix log: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/log#> .
@prefix math: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/math#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

# The candidates. The log:table statement is a hint some reasoners use to
# remember answers to recursive questions; no rule here reads it.
_:b1 log:table :moreInterestingThan .
:A :score 8 .
:B :score 5 .
:B :moreInterestingThan :C .
:D :hasAward true .
:B :boring true .

# Backward rule 1: X is more interesting than Y when X has the higher score.
{
    ?X :moreInterestingThan ?Y .
} <= {
    ?X :score ?SX .
    ?Y :score ?SY .
    ?SX math:greaterThan ?SY .
} .

# Backward rule 2: a candidate with an award is more interesting than a
# boring one.
{
    ?X :moreInterestingThan ?Y .
} <= {
    ?X :hasAward true .
    ?Y :boring true .
} .

# Backward rule 3: the comparison is transitive. If X beats Y and Y beats Z,
# then X beats Z.
{
    ?X :moreInterestingThan ?Z .
} <= {
    ?X :moreInterestingThan ?Y .
    ?Y :moreInterestingThan ?Z .
} .

# Forward rule: ask the question and record the answer.
{
    :A :moreInterestingThan :C .
} => {
    :A :moreInterestingThan :C .
} .

# Query: select that answer as the output.
{
    :A :moreInterestingThan :C .
} log:query {
    :A :moreInterestingThan :C .
} .
